    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="0967" n="936" sameAs="frus:frus1981-88v44p1/pg936"/>

                <head>263. Memorandum From Robert Linhard,
                        William Heiser, and William Tobey of the National Security Council
                    Staff to the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs (Powell)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d263-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1988-07-02">July 2, 1988</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>NSPG, Wednesday, July 6</item>
                    <item>Handling the Krasnoyarsk Radar Issue</item>
                </list>
                <p>Attached at <hi rendition="#italic">Tab I</hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d263-f2"/> is
                    a short memorandum for the President which provides him with:</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Tab A</hi> an NSC-developed issues paper;<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d263-f3"/></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Tab 1</hi> the letter from twenty Senators on this
                        issue;<anchor n="4" xml:id="d263-f4"/> and,</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Tab 2</hi> a paper containing 2 options for handling the
                        situation.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d263-f5"/></p>
                <p>Attached at <hi rendition="#italic">Tab II</hi> is a set of talking points
                    drafted for your consideration. They track the paper at <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tab A</hi>. In using these points, we would like to preposition the options
                    paper (<hi rendition="#italic">Tab 2</hi>) so that it is available in front of
                    each participant. Unless we hear otherwise, we will discuss these two options
                    with the ACSG on Tuesday (10:00 a.m. – noon)
                    so that the majority of the NSPG principals
                    should be familiar with the options <hi rendition="#italic">before</hi> the
                    meeting.</p>
                <pb facs="0968" n="937" xml:id="pg937"/>
                <p>Also, in developing these points, we have suggested an order in which we would
                    recommend that you attempt to take comments at the NSPG. The order is designed to ensure: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>that all those with strong feelings have a chance to express
                            themselves; and,</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>that the flow most likely supports a proper outcome.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p rendition="#section-heading #italic">Recommendations</p>
                <p>That you approve and forward the memorandum to the President at <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Tab I</hi> (with the material at <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tabs 1 and 2</hi>), and that you approve the approach to the NSPG suggested by the talking points drafted for
                    your use at Tab II.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d263-f6"/></p>
                <p>Concurrence: None. Neither Rostow nor
                        Fortier were available as this was
                    drafted. We will provide copies to both on Tuesday morning.</p>


                <xi:include href="d263-a1.xml" xpointer="d263-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
